#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""aequalyn-watchdog -- the Aequalyn shell supervisor and plugin sandbox.

QuickShell runs every plugin of the Aequalyn desktop in one process, which
means one broken plugin (a segfault, a hang, a leak) can take the whole
shell down with it. This program is the missing watchdog:

  * it runs the main shell and restarts it if it crashes, hangs (an
    unanswered `qs ipc call watchdog ping`) or outgrows its memory budget;
  * it runs marked plugins OUT OF PROCESS, each as its own QuickShell
    instance (see tools/watchdog/host/shell.qml), so a failing plugin
    loses only its own process. A plugin that keeps failing is turned off
    in Settings (a message in the corner says why) instead of being
    restarted forever;
  * it enforces a RAM budget per process, sampling /proc, children
    included;
  * it is the one gate to root: a plugin that wants to run as root must
    DECLARE the actions up front (needsRoot in its manifest, the command
    pinned there), ask through `aequalyn-watchdog exec-root`, and have the
    user approve each run in a window that names the plugin and the exact
    command. Anything that looks like sudo/pkexec outside the gate is
    stopped and counted like any other fault. It refuses to run as root
    itself: the whole chain is a user session, by design.

Which plugins are sandboxed:
  * built-ins marked `sandbox: true` in Plugins.js;
  * Store (third-party) plugins whose manifest group is "background";
  * anything listed in "forceSandbox" in ~/.config/aequalyn/watchdog.json;
  * minus anything in "noSandbox" there (also pushed into the shell, so
    the in-process loader picks those plugins back up).

The daemon talks to its CLI over a unix socket, and to the shell over the
shell's own IPC (`qs ipc call watchdog ...`): plugin toggles made anywhere
(Settings page, CLI, an automatic turn-off) all end up in the same place,
the `plugins` list in ~/.config/aequalyn/settings.json.

Stdlib only. See tools/watchdog/README.md for the full contract.
"""

